          dealing: 'Cards are dealt out; a bidding phase follows.',
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Be the first player or partnership to reach the agreed target score (25 in original McLoughlin rules, or 40 in many editions) by winning tricks and scoring points from the Lost Heir card (+5) while avoiding the Wrong Boy penalty (-3); blank cards cancel the Heir or Wrong Boy when played to the same trick.',
          gameplay: '1. **Bidding**: Players bid for the right to choose the Trump city. The bid is the number of tricks they expect to win (plus bonus cards). 2. **Special Cards**: The Lost Heir (highest trump, +5 points), The Wrong Boy (penalty card, -3 points), Blank City Cards (can negate points). 3. **Trick Play**: Must follow suit. If unable, play any card (including trump). Highest card of led suit wins, or highest trump. Each trick = 1 point.',
          keyRules: [
            'Bid for right to choose Trump city.',
            'Lost Heir = +5 points (highest trump).',
            'Wrong Boy = -3 points (penalty card).',
            'Blank City Cards can negate Heir/Boy points.',
            'Each trick = 1 point.',
            'First to 40 points wins.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "Only bid if you have 'The Mayor' of the city you want to name as trumps. Without the Mayor, your opponents can easily steal your trumps and force you into 'The Wrong Boy' traps.",
          intermediate: "The Wrong Boy Dump: If you are the declarer and you have 'The Wrong Boy', lead it on a trick where your partner has a high card but the opponents have already followed suit. This 'buries' the penalty in your own team's pile safely.",
          advanced: "Blank Card Counter: Save your 'Blank' cards for the final few tricks. If the opponents haven't played 'The Lost Heir' yet, they are likely holding it for the last trick. Use your Blank to turn their +5 point victory into a standard 1-point trick.",
          tips: [
            "New York is traditionally the 'preferred' suit in many editions; if bids are tied, New York wins.",
            "Pay attention to the 'Judge' and 'Policeman' ranksâ€”they are the key to exhausting the opponents' smaller city cards.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
